1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,500
Extreme power. Extreme speed.

2
00:00:04,501 --> 00:00:09,600
From the X-1 that broke the sound barrier to the scramjet aiming for Mach 10.

3
00:00:09,601 --> 00:00:14,800
Planes on the cutting edge. From the past, the present and the future.

4
00:00:14,801 --> 00:00:18,600
Now, Xtreme Aircraft on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,920
In March of 2004, a test plane mounted on a rocket carried aloft by a bomber made history at Edwards Air Force Base, east of Los Angeles.

6
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,040
Launch, launch, launch.

7
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:56,940
After release from its B-52 mothership, the small unmanned X-43 flew at the amazing speed of Mach 7.

8
00:00:56,941 --> 00:01:00,580
faster than any known jet aircraft has ever flown.

9
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:03,040
It was a very significant event.

10
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:07,380
Some people have even gone so far as to equate it actually with the Wright Brothers flight.

11
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:13,160
When it separated from the rocket, the X-43 accelerated on its own power to Mach 7.

12
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:17,060
Mach 7 is approximately 5,000 miles per hour,

13
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:21,180
so you can imagine flying New York to Los Angeles in just over a half an hour.

14
00:01:21,181 --> 00:01:29,360
The NASA Dryden Flight Research Center says the scramjet-powered X-43 is capable of reaching at least Mach 10.

15
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:34,040
A scramjet is a supersonic combustion ramjet.

16
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:43,080
The ramjet, sometimes called the flying stovepipe, is one of the simplest engine designs ever conceived, with virtually no moving parts.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,800
It's basically a hollow tube into which fuel is injected and ignited.

18
00:01:47,801 --> 00:01:52,740
Unlike regular jets, there are no compressor blades inside to compress the air.

19
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:59,360
In a ramjet, supersonic air is slowed down to subsonic speed inside the jet engine.

20
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:04,000
But in a scramjet, the air moves through the engine at supersonic speed.

21
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:12,240
The X-43 vehicle has to endure some very extreme temperatures.

22
00:02:12,241 --> 00:02:18,400
The temperature along the leading edges of the vehicle, for example, are in excess of 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

23
00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:24,980
The front of the X-43 and the leading edges of the tail are made of carbon carbon,

24
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:30,360
a lightweight and strong composite material that can withstand extremely high temperatures.

25
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:34,780
Carbon carbon that we use is sort of a high-tech cousin to carbon fiber.

26
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:38,520
The carbon itself is different. It's a much higher temperature.

27
00:02:38,521 --> 00:02:40,660
The bonding agent is much higher temperature.

28
00:02:40,661 --> 00:02:44,140
It's good to 3,000 degrees without degrading. It doesn't wear away.

29
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:48,240
What you see is the front end of the vehicle, the tungsten nose.

30
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:53,380
The very front edge is the carbon-carbon leading edge that provides thermal protection.

31
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
As you can see, there's a lot of complicated machinery

32
00:02:57,001 --> 00:03:02,160
and very densely packed systems inside this vehicle in order to make it work.

33
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:08,340
A number of small electric motors on the aircraft are controlled by computer signals from the ground.

34
00:03:08,341 --> 00:03:13,300
Motors on these electric actuators move the surfaces.

35
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:16,440
That's how we maintain control of the vehicle in flight.

36
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:20,000
Ignition. Ignition.

37
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:23,800
Keeping the fuel burning inside the engine at supersonic speeds

38
00:03:23,801 --> 00:03:27,580
has been described as trying to keep a match lit in a hurricane.

39
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:33,300
The flame doesn't really blow out because there are areas in the engine,

40
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:35,260
small areas that aren't supersonic,

41
00:03:35,261 --> 00:03:38,940
that allow a flame to grab hold and to sustain itself.

42
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:44,120
There's something about flying that fast that I think just captures the imagination of folks.

43
00:03:44,460 --> 00:03:47,500
It's just phenomenal to imagine flying that fast.

44
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:52,360
I'd say the main interest you find for the scramjet in the near term would be the military.

45
00:03:52,361 --> 00:03:59,000
They would like to have a missile that goes, say, Mach 5, Mach 6, five or six times the speed of sound,

46
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:03,000
so it can get to a target very quickly and has a lot of range in doing that.

47
00:04:03,001 --> 00:04:07,140
You can do that now with a solid rocket motor, but it runs out of gas pretty quickly.

48
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:13,320
The Air Force is also interested in using scramjet propulsion for super-fast bombers

49
00:04:13,321 --> 00:04:16,640
that could strike anywhere in the world with amazing speed,

50
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:19,500
flying more than ten times as fast as current bombers.

51
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:24,600
There's no reason that a human couldn't pilot or be a passenger on a hypersonic airplane.

52
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:28,920
Vision vehicles, as we call them, include space access vehicles

53
00:04:28,921 --> 00:04:33,360
to take astronauts to the space station or to orbit,

54
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:36,400
and also potentially passenger vehicles, again,

55
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:38,560
to take people to the other side of the world.

56
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:44,000
Some experts believe the scramjet could usher in a whole new era in aviation.

57
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:48,740
We entered the 19th century moving at 6 miles an hour,

58
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:50,320
the speed of an animal-drawn vehicle.

59
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:53,600
We entered the 20th century moving at 60 miles an hour,

60
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:55,800
the speed of a steam locomotive.

61
00:04:55,801 --> 00:04:58,320
We entered the 21st century at 600 miles an hour,

62
00:04:58,321 --> 00:04:59,980
the speed of an intercontinental jet airliner.

63
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:02,980
And if you plot this in semi-logarithmic fashion,

64
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:06,860
you get a nice straight line that indicates we may well enter the next century

65
00:05:06,861 --> 00:05:10,620
at 6,000 miles an hour, which is precisely where you see

66
00:05:10,621 --> 00:05:14,800
the X-43 really pointing us and heading in that direction.

67
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:20,800
The X-43 scramjet is carrying on a tradition of cutting-edge aircraft development

68
00:05:20,801 --> 00:05:22,180
that goes back decades.

69
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:26,680
And although Mach 7 is a major accomplishment for an air-breathing jet engine,

70
00:05:26,681 --> 00:05:30,780
Rocket-powered planes were flying almost that fast decades ago.

71
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:37,680
It all began with the most amazing series of extreme aircraft in aviation history.

72
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:42,300
They were called the X-planes, the X standing for experimental.

73
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:46,940
The very first X-plane was appropriately named the X-1.

74
00:05:46,941 --> 00:06:00,740
In 1944, the United States decided to bite the bullet really and develop a specialized

75
00:06:00,741 --> 00:06:05,120
family of research airplanes of which the X-1 was first that would essentially use the

76
00:06:05,121 --> 00:06:10,320
sky as a laboratory and which would carry 500 pounds of recording instrumentation and

77
00:06:10,321 --> 00:06:15,440
at the same time would also radio very important values that were measured in flight down to

78
00:06:15,441 --> 00:06:16,940
ground stations.

79
00:06:16,941 --> 00:06:23,080
This marked the birth, really, of a remarkable family of airplanes that continues to the present day, the so-called X-Series.

80
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:28,400
The Bell X-1 is arguably the most famous pure research aircraft ever built.

81
00:06:29,640 --> 00:06:34,720
Bell aircraft built three X-1 rocket planes for the primary purpose of breaking the sound barrier.

82
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:45,100
For decades, traveling faster than the speed of sound, about 660 miles an hour at high altitude, it seemed an impossible task.

83
00:06:45,101 --> 00:06:46,600
hence the term barrier.

84
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:50,820
It literally was seen by many as an impenetrable wall.

85
00:06:52,040 --> 00:06:55,660
This was really brought home in September 1946

86
00:06:55,661 --> 00:06:58,520
when Britain's leading test pilot, Geoffrey de Havilland,

87
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:00,920
the son of the founder of the de Havilland Company,

88
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:04,160
was killed at about eight-tenths the speed of sound

89
00:07:04,161 --> 00:07:07,740
flying a new swept-wing, tailless research airplane

90
00:07:07,741 --> 00:07:09,540
called the de Havilland Swallow.

91
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:12,740
The airplane began to pitch violently out of control

92
00:07:12,741 --> 00:07:14,780
so rapidly that he could not control it,

93
00:07:14,781 --> 00:07:18,480
and within less than a second it had disintegrated and he was killed.

94
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:21,860
This showed that the sound barrier had very real bite.

95
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:27,840
In 1947, a young Air Force pilot named Chuck Yeager was about to bite back.

96
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:31,000
It would happen at the Air Force Base at Muroc, California,

97
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:33,540
about 80 miles east of Los Angeles.

98
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:36,480
We know it now as Edwards Air Force Base.

99
00:07:38,420 --> 00:07:40,860
Robert Cardenas, another young fighter pilot,

100
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:43,060
was officer in charge on the X-1 program,

101
00:07:43,061 --> 00:07:46,180
and he piloted the bomber that carried the X-1 aloft.

102
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:50,820
I had professors from Caltech and MIT that were out there at Miroc.

103
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:56,100
We had all sorts of people making recommendations.

104
00:07:56,640 --> 00:08:02,000
And one of them said, you know, really, we've seen stuff coming in from space explode.

105
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:10,300
Whether it was from heat or what, there could be an ultrasonic barrier out there

106
00:08:10,301 --> 00:08:14,960
that might just disintegrate or melt the aircraft.

107
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:18,460
The test pilots and engineers listened politely to the experts

108
00:08:18,461 --> 00:08:20,260
and then got down to business.

109
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:24,080
Bell Aircraft test pilot Chalmers Goodland

110
00:08:24,081 --> 00:08:28,160
made the first powered flight in an X-1 aircraft in late 1946.

111
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:31,720
But it would be Chuck Yeager who 10 months later

112
00:08:31,721 --> 00:08:35,420
would take the X-1 rocket plane to the speed at which it was meant to fly.

113
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:37,520
Chuck is a human being,

114
00:08:37,521 --> 00:08:45,240
But when he gets in that seat and he locks that door, he becomes part of the machine.

115
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:47,020
In my book, he's no longer a human.

116
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:48,420
He's part of that.

117
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:50,340
He melds in as part of that machine.

118
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:55,560
Man and machine finally broke through the sound barrier in October of 1947.

119
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:59,460
On the ground, observers heard the crack of a sonic boom.

120
00:08:59,461 --> 00:09:07,280
Air Force Captain Chuck Yeager and the X-1

121
00:09:07,281 --> 00:09:10,320
had just launched a new era of supersonic flight.

122
00:09:11,460 --> 00:09:13,880
Chuck was the X-1, period. That was it.

123
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:17,380
And I really don't know if anyone else could have done it.

124
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:20,360
High air speeds are described in Mach numbers

125
00:09:20,361 --> 00:09:24,400
using a formula named after an Austrian physicist, Ernst Mach.

126
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,340
The speed of sound is Mach 1,

127
00:09:27,341 --> 00:09:31,640
And Jaeger had reached Mach 1.06 on that historic flight.

128
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:36,200
Later generations of X-1 aircraft would fly twice that speed.

129
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:45,040
In 1953, Chuck Jaeger took one of them to Mach 2.44.

130
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:54,700
Many more X-planes would follow the X-1, but none would provide more crucial research data than the X-15.

131
00:09:57,340 --> 00:10:10,340
The three rocket-powered X-15 aircraft flew 199 flights from 1963 to 1967.

132
00:10:10,341 --> 00:10:16,840
Far beyond supersonic, these research planes ushered in the era of hypersonic flight, the

133
00:10:16,841 --> 00:10:20,840
term for speeds greater than Mach 5.

134
00:10:20,841 --> 00:10:28,860
We had a 57,000-pound thrust rocket engine that was completely throttlable, the first of its kind,

135
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:33,200
and you had tremendous problems in simply making this a reliable propulsion system.

136
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:39,920
Now, once we did, all speed and altitude records very quickly were eclipsed by this airplane.

137
00:10:39,921 --> 00:10:45,640
It went out and immediately took us, in a piloted sense, to Mach 4, Mach 5, Mach 6.

138
00:10:45,641 --> 00:10:58,863
As rocket planes like the X-1 and X-15 made amazing progress pushing the limits of speed, they paved the way for a new, top-secret aircraft that, to this day, inspires

139
00:10:58,864 --> 00:10:59,564
awe.

140
00:10:59,940 --> 00:11:01,920
The SR-71 Blackbird.

141
00:11:04,460 --> 00:11:12,820
In 1967, one of the X-15s set a speed record for manned flight in a winged aircraft, exceeded only by the space shuttle.

142
00:11:12,821 --> 00:11:15,820
Extreme Aircraft will return on Modern Marvels.

143
00:11:15,821 --> 00:11:19,820
We now return to Extreme Aircraft on Modern Marvels.

144
00:11:21,820 --> 00:11:28,820
Cloaked in secrecy, two American spy planes, the U-2 and the SR-71 Blackbird,

145
00:11:28,821 --> 00:11:31,820
made their mark on history during the Cold War.

146
00:11:31,821 --> 00:11:35,820
The U-2 was the first to become operational in 1956.

147
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:49,320
Built originally for the CIA by the Lockheed Advanced Development Projects Group, better

148
00:11:49,321 --> 00:11:54,500
known as the Skunk Works, the U-2 could fly higher than any aircraft in existence at the

149
00:11:54,501 --> 00:12:01,000
time and provide a crucial intelligence on Soviet military activities.

150
00:12:01,001 --> 00:12:05,880
When there was worry that the Soviet Union might be gathering a bomber fleet or might

151
00:12:05,881 --> 00:12:11,520
be gathering its forces for an attack on Western Europe or whatever else it might be up to,

152
00:12:11,521 --> 00:12:16,340
There was no way, literally, to penetrate this iron curtain, say fly over it.

153
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:23,020
To operate at 70,000 feet and higher, the U-2's jet engine had to be virtually hand-built,

154
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:27,320
with much closer tolerances to reduce air pressure losses at high altitudes.

155
00:12:29,420 --> 00:12:34,760
Playing long transatlantic missions alone at night, navigating only by the stars with a sextant,

156
00:12:34,761 --> 00:12:41,260
the U-2 pilots collected intelligence that dramatically improved America's ability to assess the Soviet threat.

157
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:46,520
The U-2 operations really gave rise to some remarkable images.

158
00:12:46,521 --> 00:12:51,520
There was imagery brought back that showed Soviet fighters trying to climb and catch it,

159
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:54,520
stalling, spinning out of control, falling to earth,

160
00:12:54,521 --> 00:12:58,520
other airplanes trying to do so-called pop-ups to get to altitude.

161
00:12:58,521 --> 00:13:02,520
Clearly, they knew we were there, but they were very frustrated

162
00:13:02,521 --> 00:13:04,520
at not being able to reach us with airplanes.

163
00:13:04,521 --> 00:13:10,520
The long, wide, straight wings of the U-2 gave the plane glider-like characteristics.

164
00:13:10,521 --> 00:13:18,360
And it would soar so far above the ground that even if the Soviet military detected it, they couldn't reach it with fighters or missiles.

165
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:23,200
Or so it was believed until May 1st, 1960.

166
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:30,900
On that day, Francis Gary Powers was shot down by Soviet missiles as he took photos from 67,000 feet.

167
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:36,860
After Powers successfully ejected, the Soviets put him on trial for espionage.

168
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:40,580
The incident severely strained U.S.-Soviet relations.

169
00:13:41,580 --> 00:13:43,460
Powers was released two years later.

170
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:48,680
Today, high-altitude reconnaissance is done mainly by satellites,

171
00:13:49,240 --> 00:13:56,800
but an updated version of the U-2 has provided battlefield intelligence in Bosnia, Kosovo, Afghanistan, and Iraq.

172
00:13:56,801 --> 00:14:02,320
It is also being used for high-altitude research projects by NASA and other civilian agencies.

173
00:14:03,020 --> 00:14:04,840
We do earth sciences.

174
00:14:04,841 --> 00:14:07,780
NASA has done it for 30 years or more.

175
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:19,400
And that requires overflight, over forest fires, over forests, desert, into the Arctic to check on the ozone layer, which NASA first discovered.

176
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:25,900
We fly a bigger variant of the U-2, but we have two of them, and we fly them worldwide on various missions of those kinds.

177
00:14:26,980 --> 00:14:34,060
As effective and enduring as the U-2 has been, it wasn't enough to meet America's reconnaissance needs at the height of the Cold War.

178
00:14:34,840 --> 00:14:42,720
Once again, the Lockheed Skunk Works, headed by the legendary Kelly Johnson, was tapped to come up with something better.

179
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:47,000
And in 1962, they outdid themselves.

180
00:14:47,001 --> 00:15:01,001
the sr-71 blackbird a high-speed high-altitude spy plane is revered by many as one of the greatest

181
00:15:04,500 --> 00:15:11,500
jet planes ever built the ethereal blackbird could fly higher and faster than any jet aircraft in

182
00:15:11,501 --> 00:15:15,300
production and that still holds today.

183
00:15:15,301 --> 00:15:18,563
I knew that this airplane was going to be the fastest in the world.

184
00:15:18,564 --> 00:15:19,440
What I didn't know

185
00:15:19,441 --> 00:15:22,980
was it would remain the fastest for forty years.

186
00:15:22,981 --> 00:15:26,480
That's astonishing, but I remember back in the sixties

187
00:15:26,481 --> 00:15:30,520
Kelly used to say, and he'd say it kind of aggressively, like I challenge anyone to

188
00:15:30,521 --> 00:15:34,540
adversely comment on this, he says, nobody's going to produce an airplane with

189
00:15:34,541 --> 00:15:38,940
greater performance than this one by the year 2000 and back then I used to think

190
00:15:38,941 --> 00:15:42,940
Well, I hope I hang around long enough for the year 2000 to see if that turns out to be accurate,

191
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:47,480
because before this came along, every two or three years, somebody somewhere would produce one faster.

192
00:15:49,080 --> 00:15:53,580
As with the U-2, the initial client was the CIA, not the Air Force.

193
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:59,320
The first version was called the A-12 and made its first flight in spring of 1962.

194
00:15:59,321 --> 00:16:04,560
To be able to withstand the heat generated by sustained speeds above Mach 3,

195
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:08,660
the A-12 had to be designed from scratch with new technology.

196
00:16:08,940 --> 00:16:10,020
and new materials.

197
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:16,080
We would say this is roughly an 800 degree Fahrenheit airplane.

198
00:16:16,700 --> 00:16:19,080
Some things are hotter, but we have some cooler.

199
00:16:19,740 --> 00:16:24,540
I understand a self-cleaning oven is around 425 and a soldering iron around 550.

200
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:27,700
So that gives you an idea of what kind of heat we're talking here.

201
00:16:28,520 --> 00:16:32,020
The airframe was made from titanium and titanium alloys,

202
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:35,760
and the structure was coated with a special radar-absorbing black paint

203
00:16:35,761 --> 00:16:37,360
that helped dissipate the heat.

204
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:47,080
Because the aircraft surface was designed to expand from the heat during flight, the plane would leak fuel while sitting on the ground.

205
00:16:48,220 --> 00:16:52,520
The expansion would cause the entire aircraft to grow a couple of inches in length.

206
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:57,720
This is the ejection sequence initiator.

207
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:02,940
And you're flying along and the canopy is closed and you want to get out.

208
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:04,520
So you pull this thing up.

209
00:17:04,820 --> 00:17:06,900
The first thing that happens is this canopy goes.

210
00:17:06,901 --> 00:17:11,700
and then up you go and as you come out you turn like this and then you're dynamic this way and

211
00:17:11,701 --> 00:17:17,960
you deploy a stability chute, a small chute just for stability and the G's may knock you out but

212
00:17:17,961 --> 00:17:23,480
it won't be a death concern on that and as you're coming down you may still be unconscious and

213
00:17:23,481 --> 00:17:28,860
and then when you hit 15,000 feet it cuts away that baby drogue chute for stability and deploys

214
00:17:28,861 --> 00:17:30,936
a large 35 foot chute.

215
00:17:30,937 --> 00:17:36,880
The aircraft was about 100 feet long and could carry 20 tons of

216
00:17:36,881 --> 00:17:42,400
special JP-7 jet fuel in the fuselage and wing tanks, enough to fly for about two hours.

217
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:49,780
During this time, the high-flying spy plane could survey about 200,000 square miles of the Earth's

218
00:17:49,781 --> 00:17:50,533
surface.

219
00:17:50,534 --> 00:17:58,440
The CIA's highly classified single-seat A-12 version first flew in 1962, and the Air

220
00:17:58,441 --> 00:18:02,485
Force's twin-seat SR-71 version first flew in 1964.

221
00:18:02,486 --> 00:18:05,420
The two planes were nearly identical,

222
00:18:05,421 --> 00:18:10,820
and both could fly faster than Mach 3 at heights above 85,000 feet.

223
00:18:11,900 --> 00:18:15,380
The CIA continued to fly the A-12 until 1968,

224
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:18,700
when the Air Force Blackbirds, based at Edwards Air Force Base,

225
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:20,460
took over their reconnaissance duties.

226
00:18:21,960 --> 00:18:25,540
It wasn't only the fastest and highest-flying airplane of its day.

227
00:18:25,680 --> 00:18:28,760
It also pioneered what today is called stealth,

228
00:18:29,180 --> 00:18:31,200
the ability to evade detection by radar.

229
00:18:31,201 --> 00:18:37,100
That airplane has these very blended shapes and sharp chimes,

230
00:18:37,340 --> 00:18:40,220
and it's got the tails folded in and so on.

231
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:46,280
And they did not much by calculation, but a large amount by intuition

232
00:18:46,281 --> 00:18:48,960
and by taking it out to a radar range,

233
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:50,860
sort of the equivalent of a wind tunnel for radar,

234
00:18:51,020 --> 00:18:53,440
with models and trying different shapes and so on.

235
00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:58,220
And basically, by the time you saw the thing on radar going at Mach 3, it was gone.

236
00:18:58,221 --> 00:19:02,220
But now the Blackbird really is gone.

237
00:19:02,221 --> 00:19:07,620
The military mothballed the SR-71 fleet in 1990, claiming satellites could do the job

238
00:19:07,621 --> 00:19:08,620
better.

239
00:19:08,621 --> 00:19:14,840
Two of the Blackbirds were temporarily reactivated in 1995.

240
00:19:14,841 --> 00:19:20,300
Before there ever was an SR-71 Blackbird, another, even more futuristic-looking aircraft

241
00:19:20,301 --> 00:19:21,980
was being tested at Muroc.

242
00:19:21,981 --> 00:19:30,040
And this one was so far ahead of its time, it would have to wait decades before aviation technology caught up with it.

243
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:41,240
In 1974, an SR-71 Blackbird flew from New York to London in a record one hour, 54 minutes, and 56 seconds.

244
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:45,320
Extreme Aircraft will return on Modern Marvels.

245
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:49,920
We now return to Extreme Aircraft on Modern Marvels.

246
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:57,500
Flying Wing says it all about the appearance of two of America's most futuristic looking bombers.

247
00:19:58,500 --> 00:19:59,880
One from the past.

248
00:20:07,180 --> 00:20:08,660
And one from the present.

249
00:20:08,661 --> 00:20:21,780
Although separated by nearly half a century, they have exactly the same wingspan and were

250
00:20:21,781 --> 00:20:25,640
developed by the same company.

251
00:20:25,641 --> 00:20:30,660
The original Northrop flying wing bomber first flew in 1946.

252
00:20:30,661 --> 00:20:36,540
Its contemporary counterpart, the B-2, had its first flight in 1989 and will probably

253
00:20:36,541 --> 00:20:40,360
still be flying decades from now.

254
00:20:40,361 --> 00:20:45,320
Aircraft pioneer Jack Northrup was a champion of the flying wing design, which eliminates

255
00:20:45,321 --> 00:20:48,860
the standard fuselage and tail found on most aircraft.

256
00:20:48,861 --> 00:20:56,780
Northrup, who was a consummate master of aircraft design, the most important designer of the

257
00:20:56,781 --> 00:21:01,480
interwar period in the United States, not just because of his flying wings, but because

258
00:21:01,481 --> 00:21:07,660
he's taking all the cutting-edge developments in high-speed aerodynamics and streamlining,

259
00:21:07,900 --> 00:21:14,020
and he's putting this together, Northrop felt the next logical step was the pure flying wing.

260
00:21:14,021 --> 00:21:16,760
This became almost an obsession with him.

261
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:24,900
In 1941, Northrop was awarded a contract to develop and build the XB-35 Flying Wing bomber,

262
00:21:25,580 --> 00:21:31,340
capable of carrying a bomb load of over 52,000 pounds with a range of 7,500 miles.

263
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:39,720
World War II was over by the time the XB-35 first took to the air in 1946, powered by four piston engines

264
00:21:39,721 --> 00:21:47,660
The following year, the YB-49 Flying Wing with eight jet engines made its first flight

265
00:21:49,320 --> 00:21:57,100
Air Force Major Robert Cardenas, the officer in charge of the X-1 project, was also chief test pilot for the YB-49 Flying Wing

266
00:21:57,101 --> 00:22:02,180
Because of the plane's unconventional flight characteristics,

267
00:22:02,800 --> 00:22:06,840
Cardenas was nearly killed when he tried to put the flying wing into a stall during testing.

268
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:16,180
When you start slowing down, there's one point at which the airflow over the wing ceases to create lift,

269
00:22:16,440 --> 00:22:19,680
and you quit flying, and it shudders and it shakes.

270
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:20,940
That's a stall.

271
00:22:21,400 --> 00:22:25,680
But I was waiting for the shutter, and instead of that, it just gave kind of a lurch,

272
00:22:25,681 --> 00:22:26,720
and it went over backwards.

273
00:22:27,100 --> 00:22:29,100
and started tumbling backwards.

274
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:33,340
So I reached up and I hit the left throttle full power on the left side.

275
00:22:34,280 --> 00:22:37,660
Even though I was tumbling, this side went up, tumbled it over

276
00:22:37,661 --> 00:22:40,740
into more of an inverted spin, and I could get out of that.

277
00:22:43,520 --> 00:22:46,980
Although Northrup insisted the flying wing couldn't possibly tumble,

278
00:22:47,460 --> 00:22:48,800
Bob Cardenas knew it could.

279
00:22:49,480 --> 00:22:53,620
And he warned his good friend, Glenn Edwards, another test pilot, about the problem.

280
00:22:54,560 --> 00:23:00,160
Cardenas was apparently proven right by a tragic accident in 1948 that killed Captain Edwards.

281
00:23:01,480 --> 00:23:07,160
Edwards was testing the flying wing when it crashed for unknown reasons, killing all five members of the crew.

282
00:23:07,840 --> 00:23:16,080
I think they were stall entry maneuvers, and maybe they entered it at a little higher speed than I did.

283
00:23:16,081 --> 00:23:20,260
because on that first lurch where it goes over backwards,

284
00:23:20,580 --> 00:23:24,600
the load is on the wing like this, this kind of a load.

285
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:26,200
And that's what broke off.

286
00:23:26,380 --> 00:23:28,840
The wings broke off outboard of the jet engines.

287
00:23:29,620 --> 00:23:32,120
The last thing Edwards wrote on his notepad

288
00:23:32,121 --> 00:23:34,840
indicates he may have put the airplane into a stall

289
00:23:34,841 --> 00:23:37,440
in spite of the advice he got from Bob Cardenas.

290
00:23:37,940 --> 00:23:42,460
One of the things that they had found was one knee pad card

291
00:23:42,461 --> 00:23:45,060
which said stall entry maneuvers.

292
00:23:46,080 --> 00:23:49,940
The accident would change Muroc Air Force Base forever.

293
00:23:50,940 --> 00:23:56,840
It was renamed Edwards Air Force Base in honor of Cardenas' friend and fellow test pilot.

294
00:23:58,920 --> 00:24:07,260
In spite of severe problems with the aircraft, the YB-49 flying wing program continued, and Cardenas was developing strong misgivings.

295
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:09,640
I did not want them to buy the airplane.

296
00:24:09,641 --> 00:24:21,792
Nevertheless, in 1949, one of the planes set a new transcontinental speed record when Major Cardenas piloted a flying wing from Murok to Andrews Air Force Base near

297
00:24:21,793 --> 00:24:25,200
Washington, D.C. in four hours and 20 minutes.

298
00:24:25,960 --> 00:24:28,580
President Truman was there and was favorably impressed.

299
00:24:28,900 --> 00:24:31,040
Truman came up the cockpit and he looked around.

300
00:24:32,040 --> 00:24:33,620
He was a pretty crusty old guy.

301
00:24:33,900 --> 00:24:36,540
He used a little more foul language than I'm going to say.

302
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:38,920
He said it looked pretty damn good to him.

303
00:24:38,921 --> 00:24:40,260
He said, he's going to buy some.

304
00:24:41,140 --> 00:24:42,380
I bit my tongue.

305
00:24:42,860 --> 00:24:46,460
Then down on the ground, he turned to the chief of the Air Force and he said,

306
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:50,500
why don't you have this young whippersnapper fly this down Pennsylvania Avenue?

307
00:24:50,860 --> 00:24:53,340
Roof top level, I want people to see what I'm going to buy.

308
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:55,180
So I did. I slowed down.

309
00:24:55,300 --> 00:24:58,080
I slowed down to about 300, 350 knots.

310
00:24:59,240 --> 00:25:04,680
But I had never really realized how heavily forested the city of Washington really is.

311
00:25:04,720 --> 00:25:05,420
It's a jungle.

312
00:25:05,421 --> 00:25:09,980
Well, while going down the avenue and losing the avenue amongst the trees,

313
00:25:10,680 --> 00:25:14,160
all of a sudden I looked up and the Capitol Dome was straight ahead.

314
00:25:14,700 --> 00:25:16,460
So I had to pull up to go over it.

315
00:25:16,580 --> 00:25:21,040
Somebody on the steps took this picture as we were going over the top.

316
00:25:23,660 --> 00:25:28,300
The ill-fated trip back to Muroc would help bring the troubled flying wing project to an end.

317
00:25:29,140 --> 00:25:32,540
During the flight, six of the bomber's eight jet engines caught fire,

318
00:25:32,541 --> 00:25:35,300
forcing an emergency landing in Winslow, Arizona.

319
00:25:36,580 --> 00:25:41,080
Later that year, the Air Force's order for 30 flying wing bombers was canceled.

320
00:25:42,920 --> 00:25:46,280
But the Northrop Company wasn't finished with the flying wing idea.

321
00:25:47,500 --> 00:25:51,520
By 1981, aviation technology had changed dramatically,

322
00:25:51,900 --> 00:25:56,820
with electronic fly-by-wire controls making a flying wing design much more feasible.

323
00:25:57,480 --> 00:25:59,620
Fly-by-wire is basically computer-controlled.

324
00:25:59,621 --> 00:26:04,000
There's no hydraulic or cable linkage to the controls.

325
00:26:04,400 --> 00:26:07,420
The computer is monitoring what you want it to do

326
00:26:07,421 --> 00:26:11,780
and adjusts the flight controls in accordance with what you're asking the airplane to do.

327
00:26:12,540 --> 00:26:15,840
Northrop Grumman won a contract to build the Air Force a new bomber

328
00:26:15,841 --> 00:26:18,680
that would use Jack Northrop's flying wing concept.

329
00:26:19,940 --> 00:26:22,840
Apparently, Jack Northrop got the dimensions right the first time.

330
00:26:24,080 --> 00:26:26,420
The new flying wing, called the B-2,

331
00:26:26,421 --> 00:26:31,360
would have exactly the same 172-foot wingspan as the old flying wing.

332
00:26:32,020 --> 00:26:36,320
The build of that wing was a very, very challenging activity for us.

333
00:26:36,680 --> 00:26:39,560
The team overcame day in and day out.

334
00:26:39,640 --> 00:26:43,280
We used to call it an invention a day to get us through the day.

335
00:26:43,820 --> 00:26:47,420
And it's probably as an exciting a time as you'd ever come across.

336
00:26:48,700 --> 00:26:52,940
It's a very highly integrated aircraft, very complicated, very complex.

337
00:26:53,380 --> 00:26:54,560
She's a very smart aircraft.

338
00:26:54,561 --> 00:26:57,340
Once it's all put together, you can't do anything to her you're not supposed to.

339
00:26:57,680 --> 00:26:58,380
She'll let you know.

340
00:26:59,420 --> 00:27:04,680
Jack Northrup, who was still very alert and very much interested in flight,

341
00:27:05,120 --> 00:27:11,820
had the chance shortly before he died to learn about the existence of the B-2 program.

342
00:27:12,100 --> 00:27:14,080
And he was very touched.

343
00:27:14,180 --> 00:27:16,900
And he said now he knew why God had let him live as long as he had.

344
00:27:18,080 --> 00:27:22,120
The B-2 stealth bomber was first revealed to the public in 1988

345
00:27:22,121 --> 00:27:25,900
when it was rolled out of its hangar at the Air Force's Plant 42.

346
00:27:27,280 --> 00:27:32,400
To many, it signified that the flying wing design had finally become a successful bomber.

347
00:27:33,340 --> 00:27:37,780
To me, it was vindication for Glenn's death.

348
00:27:38,380 --> 00:27:41,000
My work and his death were not in vain.

349
00:27:41,240 --> 00:27:43,340
We did accomplish something.

350
00:27:43,341 --> 00:27:48,840
We didn't do it, but somebody accomplished the final act of making a system like that.

351
00:27:48,841 --> 00:27:55,460
The B-2's first flight came the following year, with test pilot Bruce Hines at the controls.

352
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:59,260
When Bruce Hines was going to fly it, they asked me to talk to them.

353
00:27:59,320 --> 00:28:02,180
I told them, I can't tell you anything. It's a different airplane.

354
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:04,560
Just one thing, Bruce, don't stall the airplane.

355
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:09,440
And so after he flew it, I said, Bruce, did you try stalling them?

356
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:11,740
I said, Bob, the damn thing won't let you.

357
00:28:12,600 --> 00:28:14,060
It won't let you stall them.

358
00:28:14,061 --> 00:28:21,060
The first production B-2 was delivered to the Air Force in 1993.

359
00:28:21,061 --> 00:28:26,060
The 69-foot long bombers are powered by four jet engines and carry two pilots.

360
00:28:26,061 --> 00:28:34,060
They're designed to carry 40,000 pounds of conventional or nuclear bombs and can fly at altitudes up to 50,000 feet.

361
00:28:34,061 --> 00:28:40,060
B-2s are subsonic, which means their top speed is just under the speed of sound.

362
00:28:40,061 --> 00:28:47,460
The fleet of 21 B-2s based at Whiteman Air Force Base in Missouri first proved itself in combat in Kosovo,

363
00:28:47,740 --> 00:28:55,940
where B-2s destroyed more than 30% of Serbian targets in eight weeks by flying non-stop missions from Missouri to Kosovo and back.

364
00:28:57,720 --> 00:29:04,460
The B-2 is much more technically sophisticated than America's other long-range bombers, the B-52 and the B-1.

365
00:29:04,461 --> 00:29:10,460
The B-1 as well as the B-52 are not stealthy platforms.

366
00:29:10,461 --> 00:29:17,460
Only the B-2 bomber can penetrate heavily defended airspace, fly for thousands of miles undetected,

367
00:29:17,461 --> 00:29:23,460
carrying a huge load of weapons, deliver those weapons precisely, and get out of that area without being detected.

368
00:29:25,460 --> 00:29:33,460
Jack Northrup would be more than proud, and proud of the people every bit as much as he's proud of what the people have done with the technology

369
00:29:33,461 --> 00:29:41,400
and certainly some of the very basic vision that he had years and years ago.

370
00:29:42,040 --> 00:29:43,547
With the B-2, the U.S.

371
00:29:43,548 --> 00:29:48,000
Air Force unquestionably has the most modern bomber in the skies.

372
00:29:48,600 --> 00:29:50,660
Now, they need a fighter to match.

373
00:29:51,040 --> 00:29:53,120
Enter the F-A-22 Raptor.

374
00:29:55,020 --> 00:30:00,180
In Operation Enduring Freedom, the B-2 flew a 44-hour mission with midair refueling.

375
00:30:00,181 --> 00:30:04,640
from Missouri to Afghanistan to an airbase at Diego Garcia in the Indian Ocean.

376
00:30:05,720 --> 00:30:08,220
Extreme Aircraft will return on Modern Marvels.

377
00:30:09,080 --> 00:30:12,680
We now return to Extreme Aircraft on Modern Marvels.

378
00:30:15,360 --> 00:30:20,240
The McDonnell Douglas F-15 Eagle first flew in 1972.

379
00:30:20,241 --> 00:30:32,640
and reigned unchallenged for decades as the world's premier air superiority fighter.

380
00:30:33,440 --> 00:30:38,020
But the new F-A-22 Raptor will finally bring that reign to an end.

381
00:30:38,021 --> 00:30:50,780
Both the F-15 and the Raptor fly and climb faster than conventional jet fighters.

382
00:30:51,180 --> 00:31:01,100
This enables them to dominate air-to-air battles with enemy planes, which means they can deny the skies to the enemy, hence the term air superiority fighter.

383
00:31:01,101 --> 00:31:08,480
The goal in developing a fighter airplane is really to make certain that you can totally overwhelmingly dominate the opponent.

384
00:31:09,060 --> 00:31:13,620
If you look at the F-15, it became America's most successful air combat fighter.

385
00:31:14,080 --> 00:31:18,700
Indeed, you can argue historically it's the most successful air combat fighter of all time,

386
00:31:18,940 --> 00:31:26,680
because it has never been shot down in air-to-air combat while shooting down close to 200 opponents in air-to-air combat.

387
00:31:26,681 --> 00:31:33,680
In 1975, 20 F-15 Eagles were modified to make them even faster.

388
00:31:34,680 --> 00:31:39,400
Called streak eagles, they set several time-to-climb records that still stand.

389
00:31:40,620 --> 00:31:44,640
But the new F-A-22 Raptor is expected to break those records

390
00:31:44,641 --> 00:31:47,560
and bring new meaning to the term air superiority.

391
00:31:48,900 --> 00:31:51,840
The F-15 is very old in the tooth.

392
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:54,240
You know, if we take a look at our fighter forces today,

393
00:31:54,241 --> 00:31:59,180
you realize that if these airplanes were automobiles, they'd be wearing classic car plates.

394
00:31:59,920 --> 00:32:03,940
After a testing and validation program lasting four and a half years,

395
00:32:04,380 --> 00:32:12,900
the industry team of Lockheed Martin and Boeing in 1991 won the competition to build the next generation air superiority fighter

396
00:32:12,901 --> 00:32:16,060
that would replace the aging fleet of F-15s.

397
00:32:16,500 --> 00:32:20,140
It capitalizes on a number of technological revolutions,

398
00:32:20,141 --> 00:32:26,360
And this enables us to get first look at an opponent, first shot at that opponent, and first kill,

399
00:32:26,560 --> 00:32:31,500
where we are literally moving out of the fight before the enemy realizes they're being attacked.

400
00:32:31,840 --> 00:32:37,480
You can detect him, you can fire a missile on him, and he'll almost blow up before he even knows you're there.

401
00:32:37,660 --> 00:32:41,120
So it's not that you're undetectable, but by the time he does detect you,

402
00:32:41,180 --> 00:32:46,400
you already have such an offensive advantage on him that it's really not even a competition.

403
00:32:47,280 --> 00:32:52,960
The production version of the F-22 design, now called the Raptor, first flew in 1997.

404
00:32:53,720 --> 00:32:56,940
Like the F-15 Eagle, it can reach supersonic speeds.

405
00:32:57,840 --> 00:33:00,960
But the Raptor can cruise without using fuel-hungry afterburners,

406
00:33:01,320 --> 00:33:03,120
giving an enemy less time to engage it.

407
00:33:03,720 --> 00:33:05,860
The F-822 is also easier to fly.

408
00:33:06,440 --> 00:33:09,740
An Air Force pilot who was training to fly the F-822

409
00:33:09,741 --> 00:33:13,000
told a bunch of reporters recently on the trip that I took

410
00:33:13,001 --> 00:33:18,400
that traditionally a pilot had to spend about 85-90% of his or her brain power

411
00:33:18,401 --> 00:33:20,820
just to attain a situational awareness,

412
00:33:21,040 --> 00:33:24,080
just to take the information from each of his sensors

413
00:33:24,081 --> 00:33:26,000
and understand what they were telling him.

414
00:33:26,240 --> 00:33:30,540
He says with the F-822 Raptor, that situational awareness is almost instantaneous,

415
00:33:31,040 --> 00:33:35,540
so he has most of his brain power left to devise how he's going to attack a certain target

416
00:33:35,541 --> 00:33:37,860
or how he's going to evade something, that type of thing.

417
00:33:38,060 --> 00:33:39,660
So that's a real significant difference.

418
00:33:40,420 --> 00:33:42,840
Another capability that gives the Raptor an edge

419
00:33:42,841 --> 00:33:47,980
is thrust vectoring, a system that allows the blast of hot gases coming out of the engine

420
00:33:47,981 --> 00:33:50,020
to be pointed in different directions.

421
00:33:53,200 --> 00:33:58,320
This allows the aircraft to change direction much more rapidly than conventionally powered fighters.

422
00:34:00,780 --> 00:34:06,280
Thrust vectoring was pioneered on the X-31, a NASA research plane built by Rockwell.

423
00:34:06,281 --> 00:34:14,200
The X-31 put on a dazzling display of thrust vectoring capabilities at the Paris Air Show in 1995.

424
00:34:16,260 --> 00:34:20,500
Most people at the air show in Paris when they first saw that thought the airplane was going to crash.

425
00:34:21,240 --> 00:34:25,300
And then it just flies right on out and goes and does another maneuver.

426
00:34:28,160 --> 00:34:30,740
These people had never seen it. We're just in awe.

427
00:34:30,741 --> 00:34:37,740
The X-31 helped pave the way for the FA-22 Raptor's use of the thrust vectoring concept.

428
00:34:37,741 --> 00:34:45,740
The maneuverability of the FA-22 in a dogfight is such that if you're in the merge and you're

429
00:34:45,741 --> 00:34:50,740
doing that spiraling telephone pole of death, you know, where you're going down, descending,

430
00:34:50,741 --> 00:34:56,740
losing altitude, and trying to outturn the other guy, with the FA-22, with that thrust vectoring,

431
00:34:56,741 --> 00:35:02,980
It allows you to flap, put the nose right at the guy, and fire a missile and end the engagement.

432
00:35:02,981 --> 00:35:05,900
You initially try to start fighting just like you did in an F-15,

433
00:35:06,140 --> 00:35:11,520
and then you start to realize what capabilities you have and what limitations no longer apply to you.

434
00:35:11,600 --> 00:35:14,580
So you adapt real quickly, but it doesn't take someone very long.

435
00:35:14,660 --> 00:35:19,660
After a sortie or two coming from the F-15 into the F-22, it's an awesome capability.

436
00:35:21,600 --> 00:35:25,080
The F-822 is also stealthier than any other jet fighter,

437
00:35:25,600 --> 00:35:27,740
including the F-117 Stealth Fighter,

438
00:35:28,200 --> 00:35:31,860
which represents an older generation of stealth technology.

439
00:35:33,620 --> 00:35:35,140
That's not very stealthy, right?

440
00:35:35,320 --> 00:35:37,540
But head-on, you don't see much of the jet,

441
00:35:37,720 --> 00:35:42,580
and you don't have anything hanging on the wings to give your signature away.

442
00:35:43,160 --> 00:35:45,740
And so we like to say, beware of raptors with open doors.

443
00:35:45,741 --> 00:35:48,900
When the doors are open, that means that you're shooting something,

444
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:51,060
and the door's open quick, shoot something and close

445
00:35:51,061 --> 00:35:55,020
before a radar signature gets transmitted back to the enemy.

446
00:35:55,080 --> 00:36:00,500
Even when the doors open, now suddenly you have sharp corners and weird angles,

447
00:36:00,780 --> 00:36:03,980
and that can reflect radar back in a direction you don't want to.

448
00:36:05,920 --> 00:36:10,100
The Air Force plans to eventually purchase 339 Raptors,

449
00:36:10,560 --> 00:36:13,980
and production of the aircraft is scheduled to continue to 2013.

450
00:36:16,840 --> 00:36:20,160
The F-822 is an Air Force pilot's dream.

451
00:36:20,161 --> 00:36:27,800
But there are planes on the horizon that may be more like a pilot's nightmare.

452
00:36:28,480 --> 00:36:31,200
Because these aircraft have no pilots.

453
00:36:34,040 --> 00:36:37,920
The F-15 Eagle can climb at more than 40,000 feet per minute.

454
00:36:38,440 --> 00:36:41,960
At sea level to the top of Mount Everest in 45 seconds.

455
00:36:43,180 --> 00:36:45,720
Extreme Aircraft will return on Modern Marvels.

456
00:36:45,721 --> 00:36:52,720
We now return to Extreme Aircraft on Modern Marvels.

457
00:36:52,721 --> 00:36:59,720
The Global Hawk spy plane points the way to what some believe will be a pilotless future.

458
00:36:59,721 --> 00:37:13,721
This UAV, or unmanned air vehicle, was seen in action by millions thanks to TV news coverage of its surveillance missions in Afghanistan.

459
00:37:14,720 --> 00:37:18,660
missions in Afghanistan and Iraq.

460
00:37:18,661 --> 00:37:23,540
The Global Hawk can conduct effective surveillance over an area the size of Illinois in just

461
00:37:23,541 --> 00:37:26,940
24 hours.

462
00:37:26,941 --> 00:37:32,260
It can also loiter above a specific area, transmitting almost real-time high-resolution

463
00:37:32,261 --> 00:37:35,200
images to field commanders.

464
00:37:35,201 --> 00:37:43,200
44 feet long and 15 feet high, the high-altitude reconnaissance aircraft was built for the

465
00:37:43,201 --> 00:37:45,000
Air Force by Northrop Grumman.

466
00:37:45,760 --> 00:37:48,480
Global Hawk is an autonomous air vehicle.

467
00:37:49,260 --> 00:37:53,780
For instance, when Global Hawk did a deployment to Australia,

468
00:37:54,360 --> 00:37:57,760
the Global Hawk left California with a click of the mouse.

469
00:37:58,500 --> 00:38:03,420
It is fully autonomous, and then it flew across the Pacific Ocean

470
00:38:03,421 --> 00:38:06,860
and landed in Australia some 20-odd hours later,

471
00:38:07,360 --> 00:38:10,400
at which time somebody clicked the mouse again

472
00:38:10,401 --> 00:38:13,100
and shut the engine down on the system.

473
00:38:13,200 --> 00:38:20,200
With its range of more than 14,000 miles and an ability to fly for 36 hours straight, it

474
00:38:20,800 --> 00:38:26,000
can travel to any point on the globe, conduct surveillance, and return to its base in the

475
00:38:26,001 --> 00:38:31,580
United States, all without a pilot.

476
00:38:31,581 --> 00:38:35,840
The Air Force rushed the Global Hawk into combat ahead of schedule in Afghanistan in

477
00:38:35,841 --> 00:38:42,840
in November of 2001, and it was also used in Operation Iraqi Freedom.

478
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:49,460
Although it flew only 5% of the surveillance missions over Iraq, it accounted for more

479
00:38:49,461 --> 00:38:56,460
than 55% of the information on time-sensitive targets, many of which were destroyed.

480
00:38:57,960 --> 00:39:04,960
After collecting more than 3,700 images over Iraq, the Hawk returned home in May of 2003.

481
00:39:05,840 --> 00:39:15,120
Air Force officials said the Global Hawk was over Iraq from the very beginning of the operation and had a direct impact on the Republican Guard's destruction.

482
00:39:17,140 --> 00:39:23,400
Although most use of UAVs so far has been for reconnaissance, there is growing interest in putting weapons on them.

483
00:39:24,760 --> 00:39:30,640
The Unmanned Combat Air Vehicle, or UCAV, is seen by some as the top gun of the future.

484
00:39:35,840 --> 00:39:42,560
One of the most dramatic first uses of an armed unmanned aircraft in combat came during

485
00:39:42,561 --> 00:39:47,720
Operation Iraqi Freedom, when predator reconnaissance drones were outfitted with Hellfire missiles.

486
00:39:49,880 --> 00:39:54,540
There's one famous mission that the Air Force likes to talk about during Iraqi Freedom.

487
00:39:54,880 --> 00:40:00,940
There was a broadcast tower from the Iraqi Information Agency in downtown Baghdad,

488
00:40:00,941 --> 00:40:06,460
And that tower was very close, I believe, to the Fox News tower and also to a mosque.

489
00:40:07,100 --> 00:40:11,780
And so it was a very, very dangerous target for the Air Force to go after.

490
00:40:11,920 --> 00:40:17,860
So what it did was it sent a Predator unmanned aircraft armed with Hellfire missiles into downtown Baghdad.

491
00:40:18,240 --> 00:40:23,220
And the remote pilot who was operating the aircraft from thousands of miles away in the ground control station

492
00:40:23,221 --> 00:40:30,440
was able to pinpoint that tower and actually take it out without any collateral damage to the Fox tower or to the mosque.

493
00:40:30,940 --> 00:40:38,320
The Air Force is now testing the Boeing X-45 U-Cav demonstrator, which first flew in 2002.

494
00:40:47,160 --> 00:40:54,600
The X-45 is the first unmanned aircraft that has been designed from its inception to be a combat vehicle.

495
00:40:56,140 --> 00:40:57,060
Mark impact.

496
00:40:57,061 --> 00:41:05,700
A larger version, the X-45C, is the precursor of Air Force UCAVs that will become operational in 2010.

497
00:41:07,780 --> 00:41:13,340
The X-45C will be nearly 40 feet long, with a wingspan of nearly 50 feet.

498
00:41:14,160 --> 00:41:20,900
It will be able to carry eight GPS-guided bombs, weighing 250 pounds each in its internal weapons bays.

499
00:41:22,560 --> 00:41:25,660
Northrop Grumman is also testing an unmanned combat air vehicle.

500
00:41:27,060 --> 00:41:29,480
The X-47A Pegasus.

501
00:41:35,180 --> 00:41:40,700
It was designed as a flight demonstrator for the X-47B that could be used by the Navy.

502
00:41:41,620 --> 00:41:45,800
Pegasus was our UCAB demonstrator. It was a company-funded investment.

503
00:41:46,360 --> 00:41:51,600
The way we approached the Pegasus was basically to demonstrate that, in fact,

504
00:41:51,601 --> 00:41:57,280
you could take an unmanned combat air vehicle and land it on the deck of an aircraft carrier.

505
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:07,760
There is still some debate, even within the military, about how and when UCAV should be deployed.

506
00:42:09,600 --> 00:42:15,760
I think there's just this sort of rules-the-game, ethical bridge to be crossed of is it okay for a

507
00:42:15,761 --> 00:42:21,880
drone to be, you know, and firing and are we really sure that that's Osama, you know,

508
00:42:21,881 --> 00:42:25,900
not the head of the orphanage who's walking along the street.

509
00:42:25,901 --> 00:42:28,880
There's a lot of, you know, queasiness about doing this.

510
00:42:28,881 --> 00:42:32,200
Because, you know, you're looking through a soda straw on these things and, you know,

511
00:42:32,201 --> 00:42:33,680
are you really sure that's the guy?

512
00:42:33,681 --> 00:42:37,560
The chances of making a mistake are pretty high and you really don't, obviously, you

513
00:42:37,561 --> 00:42:39,600
don't want to kill innocent people.

514
00:42:39,601 --> 00:42:44,500
We must be very, very careful that we don't develop systems that are so complex that they

515
00:42:44,501 --> 00:42:47,900
they really transgress whether or not they should be inhabited systems.

516
00:42:47,901 --> 00:42:53,200
There's a tremendous value in having the inhabited system because, to be frank about it, an inhabited

517
00:42:53,201 --> 00:42:58,400
aircraft carries with it a great deal of innate common sense that sometimes you don't have

518
00:42:58,401 --> 00:43:01,940
in a system that's relying on the lineup of ones and zeros.

519
00:43:01,941 --> 00:43:07,060
It remains to be seen how commercial airline pilots and private pilots will feel about

520
00:43:07,061 --> 00:43:11,820
sharing airspace with planes that have no pilots.

521
00:43:11,821 --> 00:43:17,980
Years ago, if any one of us got into an elevator, there was an operator, an attendant inside

522
00:43:17,981 --> 00:43:18,980
the elevator.

523
00:43:18,981 --> 00:43:24,000
Today, I certainly don't see that person in the elevator anymore.

524
00:43:24,001 --> 00:43:27,780
You get at many airports today, and you want to get to another terminal.

525
00:43:27,781 --> 00:43:30,940
You get on an unmanned train, and we accept that.

526
00:43:30,941 --> 00:43:31,940
It's normal.

527
00:43:31,941 --> 00:43:35,200
That's going to take place with UAVs, for sure.

528
00:43:35,201 --> 00:43:37,520
The technology is there.

529
00:43:37,521 --> 00:43:41,560
The will is there, and it's now just a matter of time.

530
00:43:41,561 --> 00:43:46,440
As aircraft technology continues its flight into the future, exciting possibilities are

531
00:43:46,441 --> 00:43:52,300
opening up that could change our lives and bring the world closer together, just as the

532
00:43:52,301 --> 00:43:53,300
jet age did.

533
00:43:53,301 --> 00:43:59,380
The challenge for us today is to capitalize on some of these new technologies and if we

534
00:43:59,381 --> 00:44:04,380
seize on that, we will indeed have, I think, a high-speed future.

535
00:44:04,381 --> 00:44:10,120
But if we do not, that future will be in the hands of other people.

536
00:44:10,121 --> 00:44:17,120
The P-59 era comet took off and flew into the future more than 60 years ago.

537
00:44:17,121 --> 00:44:24,120
It was a dramatic new concept at the time, but it soon became commonplace and unremarkable.

538
00:44:27,120 --> 00:44:39,120
Perhaps 60 years from now, combat jets without pilots and scramjets that fly up Mach 10 will be considered commonplace and unremarkable too.

539
00:44:40,120 --> 00:44:47,120
Nevertheless, there will always be some aircraft defined by the term extreme.

